# Austin Metro Mk2 (LC8 facelift), 1984-1990.
#
# The October 1984 facelift gave the Metro a new front end, a wider track, revised
# suspension, a new dashboard using Maestro and Montego switchgear, and — the
# commercial point of the revision — a five-door body on the same wheelbase. The
# Austin badge was dropped from the cars at the end of 1987, after which they were
# sold simply as the Metro until the Rover Metro of 1990.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Austin_Metro
# https://www.aronline.co.uk/cars/austin/metro/lc8-development-story/
#
# Dimensions 3.403 x 1.546 x 1.359 m, wheelbase 2.251 m. Source: Austin Rover
# Nederland brochure EO 172 (1984), dimension table, which agrees with the
# published 3.400 / 1.550 / 1.360 / 2.250 figures.
#
# The three-door body is modelled. The five-door was the facelift's selling point
# but the extra door division does not read at 128 px, and the existing 1980
# AustinMetro objects already cover the Mk1 shell; this model is the Mk2 only.
#
# Colours: Flame Red (BLVC 818), Zircon Blue metallic (471), Ermine White (243)
# and Silver Leaf metallic (421), all factory Austin colours of the period.
# DVSA first-registration shares begin at 1994, so the split is era-adjusted from
# the 1994-96 measured base towards red and white, which were markedly stronger on
# mid-1980s registrations, and away from silver, which was then rare: red 7,
# white 5, blue 5, silver 3. Total 20. Provisional pending the central rebase.
#
# Speed 125 km/h, matching the existing Mk1 and the Fiesta Mk4's tier rather than
# the model's ~155 km/h maximum. Comfort 50/255, on the line 180 + 1.5 x 50, a step
# above the Mk1's 38/235 for the revised suspension and one step above the Mini.
# Classes 0,1,2 as the entry-level supermini of its decade.
#
# Dates are road presence, not production: all four appear with the October 1984
# facelift and retire staggered 1995-1998 on a ten- to fourteen-year tail.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/austin-metro-mk2.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/austin-metro-mk2.dae (32 objects). References:
# citycars/references/austin-metro-mk2/ (gitignored; eight photographs from
# Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0 and CC BY-SA 4.0).
#
# Accepted low-poly deviations, recorded rather than corrected: the photographed
# rounded corners are angular at this budget, and the nose carries the common
# flush-lamp, shallow-grille arrangement rather than the Vanden Plas bright trim or
# the City X's recessed lamp units. Stage B ran in connectivity mode because the
# hatch glass sits on the cabin, above the hull's top face.

obj=citycar
name=AustinMetroMk2(FlameRed)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=7
intro_year=1984
intro_month=10
retire_year=1996
retire_month=6
speed=125
class=0,1,2
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=50
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=255

